      "knowledge_point_id": "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving for Displacement",
      "problem": "A marble is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.3\\,\\text{km/min}.$  It takes the marble $5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble falls for $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =25+(4.9)(25) \\\\  & =147.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $147.5\\,\\text{m}$ high.",
