      "knowledge_point": "Solving for Time",
      "problem": "A ball is dropped vertically downward from a height of $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the ball to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=44.1\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{44.1}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the ball hits the ground after $t=3$ seconds.",
