      "problem": "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $245\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the marble is $u=24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=245\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =(24.5)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =24.5t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50(4.9) & =5(4.9)t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50\\cancel{(4.9)} & =5\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 50 & =5t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+5t-50 & =0 \\\\ (t+10)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $t=5$ seconds.",
